{
 "thread": "QK4R90xyY80",
 "subject": "Calculating ESW with both left and right truncation",
 "messages_listed": 2,
 "last_activity": "2019-10-18T09:48:55Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "O6apUP5LAAAJ",
   "from": "Lisa Neyman",
   "author": "6c090f74098fda65",
   "date": "2019-10-16T19:16:16Z",
   "subject": "Calculating ESW with both left and right truncation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi there!<div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve been doing some literature scouring and haven&#39;t been able to find what I&#39;m looking for so I thought I might try here. </div><div><br></div><div>I am estimating detection functions using the ds() function in R package Distance for some aerial survey data, which requires the use of both a left and a right truncation. I have my models all selected but am now looking to calculate the ESW and have am struggling to determine the best approach. I understand that ESW = Probability of Detection (Pa) * Right truncation distance (W), but I&#39;m not sure how the left truncation distance comes in to play. </div><div><br></div><div>My coworker and I have come up with a couple of possible equations:</div><div>     </div><div>     1)   Pa * (Right_Trunc - Left_Trunc)</div><div><br></div><div>     2)   (Pa * Right_Trunc) - Left_Trunc</div><div><br></div><div>     3)   (Pa * Right_Trunc) / (Right_Trunc - Left_Trunc) from Supplementary Materials from<font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"> <font color=\"#000000\">Laake Jeff, Guenzel Richard J., Bengtson John L., Boveng Peter, Cameron Michael, Hanson M. Bradley (2008) Coping with variation in aerial survey    </font></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><font color=\"#000000\">                                                                                       protocol for line-transect sampling. <i style=\"box-sizing:inherit;line-height:inherit\">Wildlife Research</i> <span style=\"box-sizing:inherit;font-weight:700;line-height:inherit\">35</span>, 289-299.</font></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></font></div><div>So I guess my questions are: </div><div>     </div><div>     1) Do we need to actually incorporate the left truncation distance in the ESW equation or is it already accounted for during the ds() determination of Pa?</div><div><br></div><div>     2) If we do need to account for it, are any of the above equations correct? Or, if they are not, how would you recommend correctly incorporating the left truncation?</div><div><br></div><div>     3) Would it be a better idea to cut out all the sightings that fall within the left truncation distance from the dataset used in the ds() modeling and bypass the need for left truncation all together?</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thank you for your help!</div><div>Lisa </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "dMKDaoCJDgAJ",
   "from": "Eric Rexstad",
   "author": "f2122b34f1aa6e95",
   "date": "2019-10-18T09:48:55Z",
   "subject": "Re: [distance-sampling] Calculating ESW with both left and right truncation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n  \r\n    \r\n  \r\n  <div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\">\r\n    <div>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Lisa</p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">From a practical\r\n        point of view, I’m not sure there is additional value in ESW if\r\n        you have and estimate of P_a. The route to estimating density or\r\n        abundance can go either through ESW or P_a. I can only guess you\r\n        wish to have a value of ESW for comparison with other studies\r\n        (?). It is not obvious that ESW is informative in the presence\r\n        of left truncation.</p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">The messy bit is what\r\n        to do with the bit that gets truncated. Section 5.3.2 of\r\n        Buckland et al. (2001) provides two choices: “relocating the\r\n        line to left truncation distance (essentially subtracting left\r\n        trunc distance from all distances) or by fitting the detection\r\n        function only to data beyond that distance and extrapolating\r\n        [the detection function] back to distance zero.”</p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Mathematically ESW\r\n        also goes by the symbol mu (<img src=\"https://ci5.googleusercontent.com/proxy/rxNReCa0eODCYWIoiZocDuhBlj8TLcwkuXu_uS9w6AhewvCy-gHYSB2Hsbit1K83sMKbtt_LU6nDKgXMjxmVWkjp3i7dUA=s0-d-e1-ft#https://chart.googleapis.com/chart?cht=tx&amp;chl=%5Cmu\" alt=\"\\mu\">) defined in Eqn 5.14 of Buckland et al. (2015) as\r\n        the integral of the detection function:</p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\"><img src=\"https://ci3.googleusercontent.com/proxy/ZGdwxl11li3sw4I5_1fQWhv5qew6i-Wjf05Lj11psBnDC6BuuIWhVw1Cjnhr6EdNNb21viJbKIPJUZaKR6SWSJIpVqP08AR6zfd8mv1EKCZPA19GkJGnuicz9px08bGs8DUyeX5kLA=s0-d-e1-ft#https://chart.googleapis.com/chart?cht=tx&amp;chl=%5Cmu%20%3D%20%5Cint_0%5Ew%20g(x)%20dx\" alt=\"\\mu = \\int_0^w g(x) dx\"></p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">with the lower bound\r\n        of the integral dependent upon which course of action you took\r\n        from the previous paragraph (either 0 or left trunc).</p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Not a very useful\r\n        answer I’m afraid.</p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\"><div>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">On 16/10/2019 20:16,\r\n        Lisa Neyman wrote:</p>\r\n      <p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\"></p>\r\n      </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n          \r\n          <div dir=\"ltr\">Hi there!\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>I&#39;ve been doing some literature scouring and haven&#39;t\r\n              been able to find what I&#39;m looking for so I thought I\r\n              might try here. </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>I am estimating detection functions using the ds()\r\n              function in R package Distance for some aerial survey\r\n              data, which requires the use of both a left and a right\r\n              truncation. I have my models all selected but am now\r\n              looking to calculate the ESW and have am struggling to\r\n              determine the best approach. I understand that ESW =\r\n              Probability of Detection (Pa) * Right truncation distance\r\n              (W), but I&#39;m not sure how the left truncation distance\r\n              comes in to play. </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>My coworker and I have come up with a couple of\r\n              possible equations:</div>\r\n            <div>     </div>\r\n            <div>     1)   Pa * (Right_Trunc - Left_Trunc)</div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>     2)   (Pa * Right_Trunc) - Left_Trunc</div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>     3)   (Pa * Right_Trunc) / (Right_Trunc -\r\n              Left_Trunc) from Supplementary Materials from<font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"> <font color=\"#000000\">Laake\r\n                  Jeff, Guenzel Richard J., Bengtson John L., Boveng\r\n                  Peter, Cameron Michael, Hanson M. Bradley (2008)\r\n                  Coping with variation in aerial survey    </font></font></div>\r\n            <div><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\">                                     \r\n                                                                 \r\n                   protocol for line-transect sampling. <i style=\"box-sizing:inherit;line-height:inherit\">Wildlife\r\n                    Research</i> <span style=\"box-sizing:inherit;font-weight:700;line-height:inherit\">35</span>,\r\n                  289-299.</font></font></div>\r\n            <div><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br>\r\n                </font></font></div>\r\n            <div><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br>\r\n                </font></font></div>\r\n            <div>So I guess my questions are: </div>\r\n            <div>     </div>\r\n            <div>     1) Do we need to actually incorporate the left\r\n              truncation distance in the ESW equation or is it already\r\n              accounted for during the ds() determination of Pa?</div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>     2) If we do need to account for it, are any of the\r\n              above equations correct? Or, if they are not, how would\r\n              you recommend correctly incorporating the left truncation?</div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>     3) Would it be a better idea to cut out all the\r\n              sightings that fall within the left truncation distance\r\n              from the dataset used in the ds() modeling and bypass the\r\n              need for left truncation all together?</div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>Thank you for your help!</div>\r\n            <div>Lisa </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
